      <notesStmt>
        <note>This section of the article discusses the manuscript tradition of Prudentius' *Carmina*, focusing on the stemma codicum provided on page 80. The stemma is a basic structure, with not all witnesses (only a selection out of the 300 or more surviving manuscripts), and «with generous allowance for contamination. The text also specifies that the group CD and parents was thought by Bergman to share a parent with A; and P, with E, but with likely contamination of ACD. It would be easy to complete the stemma with this information. Shelfmarked would need to be checked in the original edition.</note>
      </notesStmt>
